INT. NIGHT BUS - ROUTE 47 - NIGHT

SUPER: "2:14 A.M."

Sodium streetlights strobe past rain-streaked windows. The bus is nearly empty. At the back, TWO TEENAGERS share one pair of earbuds, asleep against each other. Near the front, a MAN IN A HI-VIS JACKET snores, a lunchbox on his knee.

ANIKA, 40s, scrubs under a parka, sits by the middle doors, gripping a paper cup of coffee she isn't drinking.

RILEY, 20s, they/them, climbs on at the next stop with a rucksack, taps a card. Sits across the aisle.

INSERT - ANIKA'S HAND

The coffee cup trembles. Just slightly. She sets it on her knee to steady it.

RILEY
(not looking over)
(unzipping the rucksack)
Long shift?

ANIKA
(pocketing her badge)
Long enough.

RILEY
(taking out a water bottle)
You a nurse?

ANIKA
(beat)
Why?

RILEY
(shrugging, drinking)
Shoes.

ANIKA
(looks down at her own shoes, almost smiles)
Right.

The bus brakes. Hiss of the doors. Nobody gets on. Nobody gets off.

RILEY
(screwing the cap back on)
My mum was a nurse. Fourteen years nights. She used to say you can tell who's had a bad one by how they hold a cup.

ANIKA
(turning the cup in her hands)
And how do they hold it?

RILEY
(nodding at it)
Like that.

Silence. The bus rolls through an underpass. Orange light, dark, orange light.

ANIKA'S POV - her phone, face-up on her thigh. A missed call. HOSPITAL (WARD 6). Seven times.

RILEY
(leaning back, closing their eyes)
You can ring them back, you know.

ANIKA
(turning the phone over)
It's fine.

RILEY
(eyes still closed)
It isn't, though.

ANIKA
(sharp)
You don't know anything about it.

RILEY
(opening one eye)
No. I don't.

The teenagers at the back stir, resettle. The man in the hi-vis snorts and swallows mid-snore. The driver's radio murmurs a football score.

ANIKA
(a long breath)
(rubbing her thumb along the cup's rim)
There was a boy. Eleven. Appendix. Routine. I did the checks, I did all of them, twice. And I went on my break.

RILEY
(quiet)
(not moving)
Okay.

ANIKA
(not hearing them)
Twelve minutes. I was gone twelve minutes.

RILEY
(sitting up, turning to face her)
And?

ANIKA
(staring at the window)
And the one who came on after me, she saw it. She caught it. Because she was looking at the monitor.

RILEY
(beat)
(slowly)
So he's alive.

ANIKA
(nodding, once)
He's alive.

RILEY
(a small laugh, gentle)
So why are you on a night bus at two in the morning like it's a funeral?

ANIKA
(finally looking at them)
Because I wasn't looking.

The bus lurches. The cup tips. Coffee spills across her knee and she doesn't flinch. Riley takes a napkin from a pocket and holds it out, across the aisle, until she takes it.

RILEY
(softly)
(sitting back)
You looked, though. Every time you could. That's the whole job.

ANIKA
(dabbing the stain)
(not agreeing)
Mm.

RILEY
(pulling the cord for the next stop)
This is me.

The bus slows. The bell DINGS. Riley stands, shoulders the rucksack, swaying with the movement.

RILEY (CONT'D)
(at the doors, over their shoulder)
Ring the ward.

ANIKA
(not looking up)
Go home, Riley.

Riley stops.

RILEY
(a beat, gentle)
I didn't tell you my name.

Anika lifts her head. Neither of them moves. The doors hiss open onto the empty, wet street. Riley steps out and doesn't look back.

The bus pulls away. In the rear window, a small figure stands under a streetlight, watching it go.

Anika turns her phone over. Her thumb hovers, then presses. It rings once.

ANIKA (CONT'D)
(into the phone, steady)
It's Anika. Tell me how he is.

The man in the hi-vis opens his eyes and looks at her. Then closes them again.
